# tools/caravan_ladder_spine_witness.rish -- the Caravan ladder's orchestration spine is a measured number too, and the meter beside it never claimed to see this shape.
#
# Every rung of the Caravan arc holds one function that runs the whole correspondence in order: `close_the_quarrel`
# carries a position, hears a finding, answers it, takes a matter up again, walls it, calls a person about the wall,
# spares a reader who is done, and books what is owed. A rung born from the rung beneath it copies that function whole
# and inserts its own step, so the spine grows about three lines a rung -- one `try`, one `// invariant:` line, one
# assert.
#
# The copy meter beside this one counts BYTE-IDENTICAL bodies, and by that measure the spine costs the ladder nothing:
# no two rungs hold the same `close_the_quarrel`, because each is the rung below's plus three lines. That claim was
# always honest, and its scan says so at the top in as many words. What it cannot see is a NEAR copy, which is exactly
# what a staircase is -- so a thousand lines of orchestration rode free past a meter reading 47.
#
# Measured here rather than recalled, the ladder's spine is **106 distinct lines standing on disk 1,003 times across 21
# rungs** -- 897 of them a line the ladder has already written. Two independent readings agree on 897: the union count
# this scan takes, and a neighbor walk that asks of each rung how many of its spine lines already stand in the rung
# directly beneath it. A number that survives being counted two ways is a number a design call may rest on (REDS %93).
#
# This witness makes no claim about what the ladder should do next. It states the size of the thing, keeps it under a
# named ceiling with about two rungs of headroom, and reports TAME's seventy-line function ratchet where the spine has
# grown past it -- four rungs today, `refrain` longest at 86. TAME names function length a ratchet that turns on touch
# rather than a gate, so the ratchet prints and the ceiling is the only wall here.
#
# Five claims, each proven on metal:
#   1. The living ladder's spine counts clean under a named ceiling.
#   2. The spine's size is read off the ladder rather than believed -- 21 rungs, 1,003 lines, 106 of them distinct.
#   3. The copy meter's own reading stands true beside it, so this is a shape it never claimed rather than a fault in it.
#   4. The counting itself is right, proven on a two-rung corpus whose twelve lines, seven distinct and five carried,
#      are countable by eye in the fixture.
#   5. Every RED path refuses by name -- a corpus with no modules, a corpus whose modules hold no spine at all (the
#      REDS %97 shape, a count that cannot see what it measures), and a spine carry grown past its ceiling.
#
# Kin: tools/caravan_ladder_copy_witness.rish (the byte-identical meter and its three folds).
# Brief: active-designing/20260820-204641_caravan-ladder-the-spine-the-meter-cannot-see.md
# No network, no key, no funds, no device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/caravan_ladder_spine_witness.rish

say "caravan-ladder-spine: Language EN -- the orchestration every rung copies forward is counted rather than recalled, so the fold that answers it rests on a number."
say "caravan-ladder-spine: Style Radiant -- measurement beats memory; a meter reading zero over a real cost is a shape it cannot see rather than a claim it got wrong."
say "caravan-ladder-spine: Lens TAME -- safety first: the ceiling is named, the counting is proven by hand on a corpus, the seventy-line ratchet prints rather than gates, and every refusal has a name."

let living = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_scan.sh"]
assert living.ok else "caravan-ladder-spine: the living ladder scan must exit 0 -- ${living.out}"
assert living.out contains "SPINE_OK" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the living spine under its named ceiling"
assert living.out contains "SPINE_MODULES 101 holding=21" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want 21 of the 101 rung modules holding a spine"
assert living.out contains "SPINE_LINES total=1003 distinct=106 carried=897" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the spine read at 1,003 lines, 106 of them distinct"
assert living.out contains "SPINE_RATCHET over_bound=4 bound=70 longest=refrain:86" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want four rungs named past the seventy-line bound"

say "caravan-ladder-spine: living ladder -- ${living.out}"

# The second reading, computed beside the first and asserted on its own. The count above asks how many different lines
# the whole ladder holds; the neighbor walk asks of each spine how many of its lines already stand in the spine
# directly beneath it. Two different questions over one extraction, and both answer 897 -- which is what makes the
# number safe to build a design call on. A reading that agreed with the first by construction would prove nothing, so
# note what would separate them: a line dropped by one rung and written again three rungs up counts as carried in the
# union reading and not in the walk.
assert living.out contains "SPINE_NEIGHBOR carried=897" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the neighbor walk to agree at 897"
say "caravan-ladder-spine: second reading -- 897 spine lines already stand in the rung directly beneath"

# The meter beside this one, read here so the two numbers stand together. 47 carried lines is the byte-identical carry
# and it is true; 897 is the near carry and it is also true. A reader who saw only the first would conclude the ladder
# carries almost nothing forward, which is the whole reason this witness exists.
let copy = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_copy_scan.sh"]
assert copy.ok else "caravan-ladder-spine: the copy meter must still stand -- ${copy.out}"
assert copy.out contains "copied_lines=47" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the byte-identical meter still reading 47 beside the near carry"
say "caravan-ladder-spine: the meter beside it -- byte-identical carry stands at 47 while the spine carries 897"

# The counting proven by hand. The corpus holds two rungs: the foot writes a five-line spine, and the rung above writes
# those same five lines plus two of its own. So twelve lines stand, seven of them different, and exactly five are a line
# already written -- countable by eye in the fixture itself, which is what makes this a proof of the counter rather than
# a second opinion from it.
let corpus = run ["sh" "-c" "CARAVAN_LADDER_DIR=tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_corpus CARAVAN_SPINE_FN=walk_the_case CARAVAN_SPINE_CEILING=10 CARAVAN_SPINE_BOUND=6 sh tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_scan.sh"]
assert corpus.ok else "caravan-ladder-spine: the corpus scan must exit 0 -- ${corpus.out}"
assert corpus.out contains "SPINE_MODULES 2 holding=2" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the corpus read as two rungs, both holding a spine"
assert corpus.out contains "SPINE_LINES total=12 distinct=7 carried=5" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want twelve lines, seven distinct, five carried"
assert corpus.out contains "SPINE_NEIGHBOR carried=5" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the neighbor walk agreeing with the hand count too"
assert corpus.out contains "SPINE_RATCHET over_bound=1 bound=6 longest=top:7" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the ratchet naming the one spine past a bound set to bite"

# RED path one -- a corpus holding no rung modules at all.
let empty = run ["sh" "-c" "d=$(mktemp -d); CARAVAN_LADDER_DIR=$d sh tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_scan.sh; s=$?; rmdir $d; exit $s"]
assert (empty.ok == false) else "caravan-ladder-spine: an empty corpus must refuse"
assert empty.out contains "reason=no_modules" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the empty corpus refused by name"

# RED path two -- real modules that hold no spine. This is the REDS %97 shape: a scan reading a real corpus and
# answering zero, where the zero is the scan's blindness rather than the tree's virtue. Here the zero refuses instead.
let hollow = run ["sh" "-c" "d=$(mktemp -d); printf 'const std = @import(\"std\");\\n' > $d/hollow.rye; CARAVAN_LADDER_DIR=$d sh tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_scan.sh; s=$?; rm -rf $d; exit $s"]
assert (hollow.ok == false) else "caravan-ladder-spine: a corpus with no spine must refuse"
assert hollow.out contains "reason=no_spine" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the hollow corpus refused by name"

# RED path three -- the ceiling has teeth. The corpus carries five lines; held at four, it refuses, which proves the
# ceiling is a real gate rather than a printed number. The proof runs on the corpus rather than the living ladder,
# because a gate must be provable without breaking the thing it guards.
let ceiling = run ["sh" "-c" "CARAVAN_LADDER_DIR=tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_corpus CARAVAN_SPINE_FN=walk_the_case CARAVAN_SPINE_CEILING=4 sh tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_spine_scan.sh"]
assert (ceiling.ok == false) else "caravan-ladder-spine: a spine carry past its ceiling must refuse"
assert ceiling.out contains "reason=past_ceiling" else "caravan-ladder-spine: want the ceiling breach refused by name"

say "GREEN: caravan-ladder-spine -- the ladder's orchestration spine stands at 1,003 lines across 21 rungs, 106 of them distinct and 897 already written, counted two independent ways that agree, held under a named ceiling with the seventy-line ratchet reported beside it, proven by hand on a two-rung corpus, and all three RED paths refusing by name."
